WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.460
2021's "Mortal Kombat" had a long road to
production, but bankruptcies, a change in

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.349
crew, and even a global pandemic couldn't
stop the film from coming out.

00:00:09.349 --> 00:00:13.710
From its gory, R-rated content to its unique
director, this is everything you need to know

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:14.710
about the new movie.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:20.160
Every big-name property is getting adapted
into a major Hollywood blockbuster these days.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.500
Even video game movies, long seen as guaranteed
box office bombs, are starting to deliver

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.189
hits like "Sonic the Hedgehog."

00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:30.779
Given this phenomenon, it's easy to figure
out where the 2021 reboot of "Mortal Kombat"

00:00:30.779 --> 00:00:31.779
came from.

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:36.079
If a film adaptation of this video game could
deliver robust box office returns back in

00:00:36.079 --> 00:00:41.559
1995, surely an adaptation more faithful to
the source material, buoyed by decades of

00:00:41.559 --> 00:00:43.780
nostalgia, could score an even bigger haul.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:48.400
Believe it or not, the original "Mortal Kombat"
movies weren't supposed to end with "Mortal

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.400
Kombat: Annihilation."

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.899
Initially, there were plans for this take
on the "Mortal Kombat" mythos to span three

00:00:53.899 --> 00:00:54.920
whole movies.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:59.000
However, it never got off the ground, with
modern-day speculation indicating that this

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.789
was due to lackluster box office grosses and
derisive fan response to "Annihilation."

00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:07.290
Though there haven't been reports of a script
developed for this mythical third "Mortal

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.390
Kombat" movie, Threshold Entertainment tried
to build up hype for it on occasion.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:16.090
However, progress was nonexistent on this
production, which left the franchise dormant

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:17.360
for decades.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.530
Fans of the original "Mortal Kombat" movies
were understandably disappointed with this

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:25.980
outcome, and the original movie's cult following
suggests there could have been a sizable audience

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.299
for further adventures in this interpretation
of the universe.

00:01:29.299 --> 00:01:34.530
However, there's a silver lining to every
stormy cloud: The absence of an "Annihilation"

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:39.689
follow-up did help pave the way for the modern-day
reboot of the "Mortal Kombat" series.

00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:40.689
"Welcome.

00:01:40.689 --> 00:01:44.070
You're here to compete in Mortal Kombat."

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:49.400
For decades, Warner Bros. has owned everything
from DC Comics to the pre-1986 library of

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.820
MGM movies.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.729
But in 2009, the company added another major
roster of famous titles and characters to

00:01:55.729 --> 00:02:00.430
their library of projects when they bought
the intellectual properties of Midway Games.

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:05.070
This was the outfit responsible for seminal
video games ranging from "Rampage" to "Mortal

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.070
Kombat."

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:09.580
Despite leaving a massive impact on the world
of video games, the company faced extreme

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:12.000
financial pressure in the modern world.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.370
After some uncertainty over where they'd land,
Midway Games, and the entire "Mortal Kombat"

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:21.800
franchise, wound up at Warner Bros., with
the company paying just $33 million to indefinitely

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.620
own these iconic games.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:27.600
It's a fitting home for the franchise, given
that the studio had, at the time, recently

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.270
absorbed New Line Cinema.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:34.640
Among that movie studio's dense library of
titles are the two 1990s "Mortal Kombat" movies.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.240
Having already assimilated the "Mortal Kombat"
films, Warner Bros. now controls the entire

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.240
"Mortal Kombat" franchise.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:45.660
In the summer of 2010, interest in seeing
a new cinematic take on "Mortal Kombat" was

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.750
reignited by the release of a fan film entitled
"Mortal Kombat: Rebirth."

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:54.390
The project is the brainchild of director
Kevin Tancharoen, who was suddenly on everyone's

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:57.950
mind as a potential director for a new "Mortal
Kombat" movie.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:02.170
Turns out, Tancharoen was also thinking the
same thing: "Rebirth" was meant to be a test

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:06.220
film to show Warner Bros. what his "Mortal
Kombat" movie could look like.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:10.490
Tancharoen was hired in 2011 to officially
tackle a "Mortal Kombat" reboot.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:15.000
It was a big move for the filmmaker, whose
feature-length directorial efforts up to that

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.260
point were "Fame" and "Glee: The 3D Concert
Movie."

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:22.700
With initial reports claiming that filming
would start in 2012, it looked like "Mortal

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.620
Kombat" was going full-steam ahead.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:28.670
However, this new take on "Mortal Kombat"
kept getting pushed into the future.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:32.160
Eventually, Tancharoen left the production
in 2013.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.910
This left the "Mortal Kombat" reboot without
a director for a whopping three years.

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:40.650
James Wan and Warner Bros. have developed
a close relationship over the years, which

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:45.980
led to Wan signing on to produce a new "Mortal
Kombat" film in August 2015, just months after

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:49.030
directing the Universal blockbuster "Furious
7."

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:52.790
The timing of this deal doesn't just reflect
Wan's close ties to Warner Bros. — it also

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:54.910
signals the trajectory of his career.

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:59.530
"Furious 7" was a runaway success, proving
Wan could expand beyond the horror genre.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:04.650
He was also now someone associated with properly
handling big-budget action movies belonging

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:06.760
to well-established franchises.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.590
Wan stayed onboard this version of "Mortal
Kombat" as a producer during its six years

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:16.280
of production, while directing other films
for Warner Bros. including "Aquaman" and "Malignant."

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.910
You may not realize it, but you've likely
been exposed to director Simon McQuoid's work.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:25.410
Having helmed countless commercials, including
a memorable Duracell "Star Wars" ad, McQuoid's

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:28.400
visually-distinct oeuvre has been seen all
over the world.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.470
But after years in this field, McQuoid was
looking to move from directing advertisements

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.650
to helming feature-length projects.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:38.580
That opportunity came when he was picked to
direct the "Mortal Kombat" reboot.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:42.120
McQuoid's aforementioned "Star Wars" commercial
was apparently the project that put him on

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.570
the producers' radar.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:47.870
In terms of his "Mortal Kombat" approach,
McQuoid explained to Comicbook.com in 2021

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:48.870
that,

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:53.080
"everything has to feel authentic ... It doesn't
feel like there's that veneer of sort of special

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:54.650
effects over everything.

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:58.130
That for me is the ultimate sort of fantasy
of science fiction films is when you really

00:04:58.130 --> 00:04:59.130
believe."

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:03.230
After Simon McQuoid was brought on board,
news on the "Mortal Kombat" movie was quiet

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:04.230
for years.

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:09.190
However, in July 2019, exciting developments
finally began to creep in as the film's cast

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:10.190
was assembled.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:15.760
Joe Taslim was the first cast member announced,
followed by names like Mehcad Brooks and Tadanobu

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:16.760
Asano.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:20.750
From there, the cast kept growing, and a project
once shrouded in silence began to trumpet

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:21.940
its bold new actors.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:27.310
Lewis Tan, who had previously been a contender
to play Marvel heroes Iron Fist and Shang-Chi,

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:31.180
became one of the rare members of the cast
not playing someone familiar to longtime fans

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:32.180
of the franchise.

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:36.620
Tan portrays an original character named Cole
Young, who serves as the film's protagonist.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:41.591
When it came time to actually shoot the new
"Mortal Kombat" movie, director Simon McQuoid

00:05:41.591 --> 00:05:45.731
and James Wan opted to shoot the project in
a territory familiar to both of them: Their

00:05:45.731 --> 00:05:47.100
native Australia.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:51.470
When filming began in September 2019, it was
revealed that the production was engaging

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:55.480
in principal photography in South Australia's
Adelaide Studios.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:59.970
As Adelaide has largely been a shooting location
for local Australian fare, "Mortal Kombat"

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:02.720
provided a big-budget departure from the norm.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:06.310
Shooting in this location had a number of
benefits for the "Mortal Kombat" reboot.

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:10.330
For one thing, it brought the production close
to home for some of the movie's leading actors.

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:14.590
Additionally, it helped to further separate
this new reboot from the pair of 1990s "Mortal

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:15.590
Kombat" films.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:21.180
The pair of live-action "Mortal Kombat" movies
from the 1990s deliver stories that firmly

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:24.139
fit within the parameters of PG-13 filmmaking.

00:06:24.139 --> 00:06:28.680
While this allows each film to attract teenage
audiences, it also means that the gruesome

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:32.720
violence that defines "Mortal Kombat" is almost
totally absent.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.030
Over the years, this became a bit of a punchline
in discussions of the original two films,

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:41.010
as fans grappled with the quandary of how
something so crucial to the games could be

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:42.010
excluded.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:46.420
When it came time for a reboot, fans around
the world hoped for something more accurate.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:51.050
These fans got their wish, as the new "Mortal
Kombat" movie is indeed rated R. It's a rating

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:54.990
that director Simon McQuoid was insistent
upon when developing the feature.

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:57.170
In fact, McQuoid explained to "Cinemablend,"

00:06:57.170 --> 00:07:01.490
"The rules around [MPAA] ratings aren't what
a lot of people think they are.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:05.700
It's amount of blood, it's amount of red,
it's interpretation of how you go about it.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:09.260
We had a lot of discussions about getting
the balance right so there was gore and there

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:11.280
was blood and there were fatalities.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.280
And there is gore, blood, and fatalities."

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:17.900
Keeping all of that "red" at the center of
this new movie resulted in an MPAA rating

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:21.570
that promises all the gruesome carnage fans
of this franchise are familiar with.

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:25.840
"Mortal Kombat" wrapped up shooting just before
the end of 2019.

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:30.250
The film initially had plenty of time between
the end of shooting and its original January

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:31.800
2021 release date.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:35.480
However, this schedule was put together under
normal circumstances.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:40.440
As "Mortal Kombat" entered post-production
in 2020, normal circumstances were soon hard

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:41.440
to come by.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:46.320
By March 2020, the COVID-19 pandemic had totally
upended the entertainment industry.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:51.480
"Mortal Kombat" was no exception: While principal
photography was finished, reshoots had yet

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:53.830
to be completed when the pandemic took hold.

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:58.070
This was revealed in a tweet from "Mortal
Kombat" producer Todd Garner, who divulged

00:07:58.070 --> 00:07:59.070
details on the hold-up:

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:02.310
"We'd always planned for it &amp; thought we'd
do it in March.

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.080
The pandemic had other plans.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:07.350
We won't have a release date for the movie
until theaters re-open.

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:08.400
Same for trailer."

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:12.150
The pandemic clearly had adverse effects on
the production of "Mortal Kombat."

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:16.390
However, the overall impact would turn out
to be minimal, at least compared to other

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:17.390
movies.

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:20.889
"Mortal Kombat" managed to come out just three
months after its original release date, which

00:08:20.889 --> 00:08:25.150
indicates the production was able to resolve
issues in a timely manner.

